<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4472"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->PokingAround<!--/name--></b></a><br><!--field:posts--><!--/field--><!--field:registered--><!--/field--><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 22, 2005 - 5:32 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->To BTK: <BR>While reading over the excerpts from your letters one phrase in particular just happened to snag my gaze:  &#34;_no witnesses except the Vian&#39;s kids.&#34;   <BR> <BR>Funny you should refer to them in this manner, as THE Vian&#39;s kids.  One might have expected you to say Vian&#39;s kids, or Shirley&#39;s kids, or even &#39;her&#39; kids, but no, you say &#39;the Vian&#39;s kids&#39;, creating the presumption that you knew both Vian parents.  <BR> <BR>The marriage was common-law and little if any mention was ever made of the spouse.  So did you know or perhaps at some time work with Shirley&#39;s common-law husband?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
