<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5246"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->The Bard<!--/name--></b></a><br><!--field:posts--><!--/field--><!--field:registered--><!--/field--><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 28, 2005 - 7:54 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Btk, I wrote this poem for you. <BR> <BR>It was a day they all had waited for <BR>A lovely day of spring <BR>All the townfolk gathered on the lawn <BR>To hear the toll bell ring <BR>They came with their families all in tow <BR>And brought picnic baskets along <BR>Lawn chairs, coolers, umbrellas and sunscreen <BR>As well, to protect them from the sun <BR>Vendors had set up booths along the faire <BR>To tantalize the throng <BR>Sandwiches, chocolate cake and lemonade <BR>Could be had for just a song <BR>The band members tuned their instruments <BR>For soon they all would play <BR>Music to add a gay melody <BR>To the festivities of the day <BR>The murmur of the crowd began to rise <BR>A harmonious cacophony <BR>As they shaded their eyes from the noontime sun <BR>Eyes that were filled with glee <BR>For soon they had rolled the wagon out <BR>And they all strained their eyes to see <BR>The man who had brought them all here <BR>Their famed celebrity <BR>He stood before them on the platform of the wagon <BR>But yet he made no sound <BR>But stood their waiting quietly <BR>His hands and feet were bound <BR>And still they came, closer still <BR>To look in his dead eyes <BR>They stood as one, a society <BR>Their hands down at their sides <BR>And the MC came and welcomed them <BR>And made his pretty speech <BR>Applause was lifted up for him <BR>The preacher stood to preach <BR>He quoted a little scripture then <BR>He offered up a prayer <BR>And many an Amen was heard that day <BR>From those that were standing there <BR>He spoke of kindness to one&#39;s fellow man <BR>And the harsh wages of sin <BR>Then the MC rose once more to say <BR>&#34;Let the festivities began!&#34; <BR>The people gathered up their offerings <BR>For they had come to pay <BR>Their respects in full measure to <BR>The man known as BTK <BR>They gathered their stones each in hand <BR>And lined up one by one <BR>Some lifted their children onto their shoulders <BR>A rock pressed in each little palm <BR>And they hurled their stones at the monster <BR>Aiming for his eyes <BR>For each of them who hit the target <BR>Received a special prize <BR>There were congratulations and pats on backs <BR>As the monster&#39;s blood splattered and flew <BR>And each one strove to better the missile <BR>The one before him threw <BR>The baseball heroes who played in high school <BR>Showed off their winning ways <BR>And as each stone smashed into the monster&#39;s face <BR>They relived their glory days <BR>And as his screams lept from his throat <BR>Their frenzy grew the more <BR>Their hands they curled, the stones they hurled <BR>Until their arms were sore <BR>Suddenly a voice screamed, &#34;Stop, people!  Stop!&#34; <BR>And a woman ran into view <BR>She mounted the wagon before them all <BR>And called out in the milleu <BR>&#34;Look at what we&#39;ve all become! <BR>Look at what we&#39;ve done! <BR>Have all the people in Wichita <BR>Become monsters like this one?&#34; <BR>She stooped low and gathered the largest stone <BR>Raising it up high <BR>&#34;Is this what we will call justice. then? <BR>To take an eye for an eye? <BR>I know many of you, you are my friends, my neighbors and my kin <BR>Which of you standing here today can say that you are without sin?&#34; <BR>Then turning and lifting her rock up high <BR>She bashed his skull right in <BR>The crowd raised up whoops and yells and cheers <BR>And then they slowly begin to disperse <BR>The lemonade stands were soon overrun <BR>For killing is thirsty work <BR>The picnickers packed up their baskets <BR>The band dismantled its drums <BR>The townsfolk went home deliciously satisfied <BR>For BTK Day was done.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
