<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5553"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->mcattitude<!--/name--></b></a><br><!--field:posts--><!--/field--><!--field:registered--><!--/field--><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 31, 2005 - 8:37 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->This was Gary Ridgeway&#39;s deal: <BR>On November 5, 2003, Gary Ridgway, 54, avoided the death penalty in King County, Washington by confessing to the murders of 48 women, most of whom were murdered in the 1982-84 timeframe. The deal Ridgway made was to cooperate with authorities on closing these cases in exchange for 48 life sentences without the possibility of parole.  <BR> <BR>BTK bring closure for the families who do not know who killed their loved ones. If not we will never know who you really are. Even after you might die we would never know the whole story we need to be able to get the whole true story from you and that can only be accomplished by your being caught or giving yourself up. <BR> <BR>Again thank you for reading this and at least thinking about it. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
